<https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#activity/charging-2026-09-16> a prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Charging-provision constraint classification (2045)"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Companion notebook to \"Predicting EV adoption in Wales using Semantic Knowledge Graph\" (manuscript, 2026), Stage 8; paper Discussion (charging provision), Stage 8 outputs. Values taken from: companion notebook cell outputs: CLEETS_EV_Adoption_Wales_Quarterly.ipynb."@en ;
    dcterms:source "companion notebook cell outputs: CLEETS_EV_Adoption_Wales_Quarterly.ipynb" ;
    rdfs:comment "Stage 8, research question 2 of the companion notebook. Each LAD's 2045 BEV count from the turnover trajectory is set against public charging devices grown at 5% a year for 20 years ('slow' scenario) and a benchmark of 20 BEVs per public device. LADs that diffuse slowly AND fall short of the benchmark are classed as 'charging plausibly binding'; the rest as 'provision will bind later'. Sensitivity: frozen, slow and sustained (12%/yr) charger growth all leave every LAD below the benchmark. Assumption-driven; not an official forecast."@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/EVCI9001>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH0105>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH0132> ;
    cleets:notebookStage "Stage 8" ;
    cleets:paperSection "Discussion (charging provision), Stage 8 outputs" .

<https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#activity/hierarchical-2026-11-24> a prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Hierarchical diffusion (two-stage empirical Bayes)"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Companion notebook to \"Predicting electric vehicle adoption in Wales from open data integrated in semantic knowledge graphs\" (manuscript, 2026): EVAdoptionWalesPredictionsModels24Nov.ipynb, Section 8 of the companion notebook; paper Sections 4.3 and 5.4. Values taken from: hierarchical_midpoints.csv, hierarchical_covariate_effects.csv."@en ;
    dcterms:source "notebook exports: hierarchical_midpoints.csv, hierarchical_covariate_effects.csv" ;
    rdfs:comment "Each district's logistic curve is fitted independently to its adoption share (private battery-electric cars over private cars, 2011 Q4 to 2025 Q4); the fitted midpoint and rate are meta-regressed on standardised population density and population with a DerSimonian-Laird between-district variance, and each district is shrunk towards the value its covariates predict in proportion to its sampling variance. Intervals come from a parametric bootstrap of 300 replicates. The estimates describe the fitted curves before the turnover path is applied."@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/ONSDensity>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/StatsWalesPopulation>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH0105>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH0142> ;
    cleets:notebookStage "Section 8 of the companion notebook" ;
    cleets:paperSection "Sections 4.3 and 5.4" .

<https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#activity/covariates-2026-09-16> a prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Cross-sectional covariate comparison (leave-one-out)"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Companion notebook to \"Predicting EV adoption in Wales using Semantic Knowledge Graph\" (manuscript, 2026), Stages 4a-bis, 5; paper S3.4.2 (Table 8). Values taken from: companion notebook cell outputs: CLEETS_EV_Adoption_Wales_Quarterly.ipynb."@en ;
    dcterms:source "companion notebook cell outputs: CLEETS_EV_Adoption_Wales_Quarterly.ipynb" ;
    rdfs:comment "Stages 4a-bis and 5 of the companion notebook (paper S3.4.2, Table 8). Node features per LAD (population, population density, land area; chargers and EV counts are extracted but charger features are excluded from the predictors) are used to predict EVs per 1,000 residents at the target quarter with a mean baseline, Ridge, Random Forest, Extra Trees, GCN, GAT and GraphSAGE under leave-one-out evaluation over the 22 LADs, stochastic models refit under five seeds. The seed spread is at least as large as the gap between the best model and the mean baseline, so no single winning architecture is reported. Ridge residuals rank which LADs adopt above or below what population, density and area alone would predict."@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/EVCI9001>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/ONSDensity>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/StatsWalesPopulation>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH0132> ;
    cleets:notebookStage "Stages 4a-bis, 5" ;
    cleets:paperSection "S3.4.2 (Table 8)" .

<https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#activity/neural-2026-11-24> a prov:Activity ;
    rdfs:label "Turnover-constrained neural and graph neural predictions (model comparison)"@en ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Companion notebook to \"Predicting electric vehicle adoption in Wales from open data integrated in semantic knowledge graphs\" (manuscript, 2026): EVAdoptionWalesPredictionsModels24Nov.ipynb, Sections 9 and 10 of the companion notebook; paper Sections 4.4, 4.5 and 5.5; Supplementary Table S8. Values taken from: walk_forward_backtests.csv, multi_horizon_backtest.csv."@en ;
    dcterms:source "notebook exports: walk_forward_backtests.csv, multi_horizon_backtest.csv" ;
    rdfs:comment "A multilayer perceptron and a two-layer graph convolutional network over a 22-district similarity graph predict the battery-electric share of the cars that enter private keepership in a district and quarter; the stock follows from the turnover identity, so a prediction cannot grow faster than the entering cars permit (1.48% of the private car stock each quarter, from the turnover model). They are compared with a bounded logistic, a persistence-with-drift baseline and an unconstrained perceptron under one rolling-origin protocol: one quarter ahead for every test quarter from 2022 Q1 to 2025 Q4 (352 district-quarters per model), and rolled forward 1 to 16 quarters from the same origins. The networks are evaluated only; the predictions to 2045 come from the bounded logistic capped by the turnover path."@en ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:used <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH0105>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH0142>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH1111>,
        <https://w3id.org/def/cleets/data#dataset/VEH9901> ;
    cleets:notebookStage "Sections 9 and 10 of the companion notebook" ;
    cleets:paperSection "Sections 4.4, 4.5 and 5.5; Supplementary Table S8" .

cleets:ModelMetric a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Model evaluation metric"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Walk-forward or leave-one-out error metric for one model configuration."@en .

